esents funds from private sources \n in the form of gifts \nfrom corporations, alumni, supporters, members of the governing board and friends, and the latter is institut\n ional support awarded on a competitive basis by the US \ngovernment.  \nAuxiliaries Services\n : Income from self\n -\n supporting activities, including student housing and the AUC Press and Bookstores.  \nEducation Extension services: A broad array of professional educational certificates and stand\n -\n alone courses offered to support the development needs of \nprofessionals and executives in private and public sector organizations.  \nResearch & External grants:  \nExternal funding provided by third parties for conducting scientific research  \nST Investment:  \nFinancial investments that could be easily converted into cash within the next 3 to 12 months  \nMiscellaneous:  \nIncludes income generated from other sources and supplementary activities.  \n29 \nInstruction and Academic Support\n : includes expenditures for all activities that are part of an institution’s instructional programs and for activities that d\n irectly \nsupport the instruction function, such as the Library, Deans’ offices, audiovisual and media services, and others.  \nAdministration and General\n : The expenses of all activities of administrative offices, regardless of executive level, including student services that ar\n e not related to \ninstruction and academic support  \nAuxiliaries Services: \n Expenditures from self\n -\n supporting activities, including student housing and the AUC Press and Bookstores  \nFinancial Assistance: \n Assistance offered to students both graduate and undergraduates through scholarships, fellowships, and work study opportuniti\n es\n . \nOperations and Maintenance of Plant: \n includes all expenditures of current operating funds for the operation and maintenance of the university’s physical plant. It  \nincludes all expenditures for operations established to provide services and maintenance related to grounds and facilities.  \nEducation Extension services: This category includes all expenditures related to maintaining the education extension programs\n . \nResearch/Sponsorship/contributions: \n This category includes all expenditures for activities specifically organized to produce research outcomes, whether \ncommissioned by an agency external to the institution or separately budgeted by an organizational unit within the institution\n . \nContingency, Provisions & General Reserve:  \nAll expenditures that are incurred from other uses and supplementary activities. The contingency fund is used for \nunplanned expenses.  \n30 \nThe twelve\n -\n month period of July 1 of one year to June 30 of the following year. Prior to FY2013, the fiscal year was September 1 of one \n year to August 31 of the following year.  \nRevenue\n 28 \n 202\n 6  \n Expenses\n 29 \n 202\n 6 \nAverage Foreign Exchange rate EGP: USD  \n               \n50   \n Average Foreign Exchange rate EGP: USD  \n 50  \nGross Tuition and Fees  \n 121,967  \n Instruction and Academic Support  \n 64,949 \nEndowment Income  \n 24,878  \n Administration and General  \n 34,149 \nAnnual Gifts  \n 5,000   \n Auxiliaries Services  \n 5,279 \nAuxiliaries Services  \n 4,311  \n Financial Assistance  \n 37,311 \nEducation Extension services  \n 10,395  \n Operations and Maintenance of Plant  \n 12,114 \nResearch & External grants  \n 7,577  \n Education Extension services  \n 5,321 \nST Investment  \n 1,640  \n Research/Sponsorship/contributions  \n 12,200 \nMiscellaneous  \n 2,764  \n Contingency, Provisions & General Reserve  \n 7,208 \nTotal  \n 178,531  \n Total  \n 178,531 \n     \n  \n FY21 \n FY22  \n     \n FY23  \n                 \n FY24  \n       \n FY25  \nTotal  \nEndowment  \nValue (USD)  \n 623,321,276 \n 520,528,920 \n       \n 529,483,504  \n              \n 559,495,079 \n 620,899,030  \nReturn Rate  \n 29.44% \n -\n 13.16% \n    \n6.58% \n        \n 10.65% \n       \n 13.11% \nPay